<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeremy Bentham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeremy Bentham was an English philosopher, jurist and social reformer regarded as the founder of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utilitarianism">utilitarianism</a>, which took the greatest happiness of the greatest number as the measure of right and wrong. His An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789) was the first major book on the subject. A political radical, he advocated freedom of expression, equal rights for women and the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and of capital punishment, while dismissing natural rights as nonsense upon stilts. His students included James Mill and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>, and after his death in 1832 his body was preserved as an auto-icon, now on display at University College <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham.jpg" alt="Jeremy Bentham" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry William Pickersgill · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeremy_Bentham_by_Henry_William_Pickersgill_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jeremy Bentham was born in Houndsditch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in February 1748, the son of the attorney Jeremiah Bentham, in a wealthy Tory family. He was reportedly a child prodigy: found as a toddler reading a multi-volume history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at his father's desk, he began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> at three and at seven played Handel sonatas on the violin at dinner parties. His one surviving sibling, Samuel Bentham, became a close companion. In 1786 and 1787 he visited Samuel in Krichev, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, where Samuel had conceived a circular building from which a few managers could oversee a large workforce. Bentham developed the idea as a prison design, the Panopticon, and acknowledged that the basic idea was his brother's.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bentham took as the fundamental axiom of his philosophy that right and wrong are measured by the happiness of the largest number of people, a principle he said he had borrowed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-priestley">Joseph Priestley</a>. His ambition was a Pannomion, a complete utilitarian code of law, and he coined the verb to codify; his efforts were unsuccessful in his lifetime, though they laid a foundation for David Dudley Field II's codification work in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In A Fragment on Government (1776), published anonymously, he criticised William Blackstone's defence of judge-made law, legal fictions and the social contract. He argued that the ability to suffer, not to reason, should decide who has rights, and he is regarded as an early voice for animal rights.</p><p>He favoured the separation of church and state, equal rights for women including the vote and divorce, and the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, capital punishment and physical punishment. He opposed empire, writing Emancipate Your Colonies! in 1793. In 1823 he co-founded The Westminster Review with James Mill, a journal for the Philosophical Radicals, among them John Bowring and Edwin Chadwick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bentham died on 6 June 1832 at his home in Queen Square Place, Westminster, aged 84, having written until a month earlier. His instructions, attached to his will of 30 May 1832, directed Thomas Southwood Smith to dissect his body and preserve it as an auto-icon, which is now on display at University College London. Although he is called the spiritual founder of the college, he only bought a single £100 share. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of an estimated 30 million words, now largely held by UCL and the British Library.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeremy Bentham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Romantic writers turned against what they saw as the cold rule of reason, but the Enlightenment’s ideas lived on in liberalism, in the utilitarianism of <b>Jeremy Bentham</b> and in the sciences of society.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Pacifist ideals then coalesced at the end of the 18th century from two strands, the secular Enlightenment, represented by Rousseau, Kant and <b>Jeremy Bentham</b>, and the evangelical revival that had helped the campaign against slavery, represented by William Wilberforce.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Utilitarianism as a distinct ethical position emerged in the 18th century, and although it is usually thought to have begun with <b>Jeremy Bentham</b>, some earlier writers presented similar theories.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/utilitarianism">Utilitarianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Lind's pamphlet carried an attack on natural rights by <b>Jeremy Bentham</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jeremy Bentham</b> called it &quot;the war whoop of anarchy&quot; in 1796.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1802" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Jeremy Bentham"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nature has placed mankind under the governance of two sovereign masters, pain and pleasure.&#8221;</p><footer>An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), chapter 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/An_Introduction_to_the_Principles_of_Morals_and_Legislation/Chapter_I" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;They govern us in all we do, in all we say, in all we think: every effort we can make to throw off our subjection, will serve but to demonstrate and confirm it.&#8221;</p><footer>An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), chapter 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/An_Introduction_to_the_Principles_of_Morals_and_Legislation/Chapter_I" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Natural rights is simple nonsense: natural and imprescriptible rights, rhetorical nonsense, -nonsense upon stilts.&#8221;</p><footer>Anarchical Fallacies (written c. 1796), on the French Declaration of Rights, article 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Anarchical_Fallacies;_being_an_Examination_of_the_Declarations_of_Rights_issued_during_the_French_Revolution" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
